# These run on, or are applied on, the macOS build VM. CRLF breaks both: bash
# rejects the scripts and `git apply` rejects the patches.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.patch -text

# The chart fixtures are pinned by SHA256 in LumaChart.Avalonia.Specs and in
# Test-WindowsAvaloniaSlice.ps1, so their bytes are part of the contract. Git's
# system config on Windows sets autocrlf=true, which rewrote them to CRLF on
# checkout: the blobs are LF, this working tree happened to hold LF because the
# files were never re-checked-out, and every fresh clone got CRLF and failed
# pro-editor-malody-noteref-fixture-present-with-trusted-hash on the first run.
# Pinning a hash only works if the bytes are the same everywhere.
TestFixtures/** -text

# Upstream licence texts, stored and checked out exactly as their authors
# published them. NAIST/ICOT and the OFL both require the notice to accompany
# every copy, so the copy has to be the notice -- not a line-ending-normalised
# rendering of it. Without this, autocrlf rewrote the two IPADIC files on the
# way in and the committed blobs no longer matched upstream byte for byte.
# IPADIC-COPYING.txt also carries stray trailing bytes from upstream that any
# re-encoding would silently drop.
licenses/** -text

# GitHub counts bytes per language, so the 120 GUI-capture and test-harness
# scripts under tools/ (4.7 MB) showed up as 42.8% of the project. They are
# verification tooling, not product code; this keeps them out of the language
# stats only. Nothing about the build or the scripts themselves changes. The
# build scripts under LumaChart.ClientSecurity.Native are still counted --
# they produce a binary the Android client ships.
tools/**/*.ps1 linguist-detectable=false
tools/**/*.psm1 linguist-detectable=false
